From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 22:30:19 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 60/72] dsv41 calibration: time suspect rows again before the
 table is built

The boot calibration times rows 5-8 and 9-16 back to back, so one burst on
either rank can fill a row's runs and its median. The raw table is the
monotone envelope of the rows, which carries that value to every wider row.
Cost mode then drafts shallow for the whole life of the process.

On TP2 DGX Spark this hit 15 of 54 stored rank-0 calibrations, including
three benchmark boots: H (row 6 at 67 ms against about 44), a candidate's
first boot (rows 5-6 near 60-67 ms against about 40, deep rows up to 145 ms)
and a K4 spot (row 6 at 63 ms). Each ran with code TPR 6-10% below the same
engine's clean boots.

After the reference measurement, rank 0 takes the slower rank's rows and
fits each segment's robust line (rows 2-8 and 9+, the fit shape's split,
Theil-Sen as in fit). A row more than 15% above its segment's line is
suspect, and so is a row 9+ more than 30% above the rows 2-8 line extended,
since a burst over several deep rows outvotes their own line. Suspect rows
are timed again (one warm-up round, then REPS rounds with the rows cycled)
and every rank keeps the lower of its value and the retime's median, the
recheck's rule. This repeats for at most two rounds, then the table is
built as before.

The followers need no change: they pair whatever marks the leader sends
before each gather. The reference plan, its statistics and its table are
unchanged, so the frozen golden still holds.

TF_DSV41_CALIB_RETIME (0-4, default 2; 0 is the reference measurement
only) and TF_DSV41_CALIB_SPIKE (0.02-2, default 0.15) tune the rule. The
stored entry records the retimed rows and their earlier values, and the
boot log names each one.

Tests use stored calibrations from those boots: three spiked ones flag
exactly their burst rows, three clean ones flag none, and a retime brings
the spiked row 6 back so the table loses the burst.
